"very and future audited credit remain open; the distinct decision ID awaits approval.\\n\\n- Tighten the Appendix F vector schema to require canonical fields, byte widths, integer ranges,\\n  case identities, and coverage. Wide integers use decimal strings so JSON consumers preserve\\n  their exact values; the public generator also checks duplicate identities.\\n\\n- Replace \u00a78.2's Miner and Validator airdrop unlock (R8.7, R8.8; D-0517). A Miner grant is a quarter\\n  liquid at its start and unlocks the rest one FLOP per FLOP it receives as miner from settled\\n  sessions once their dispute window passes, with no end block; block rewards earn no credit, and the performance adjustment\\n  is retired. A Validator grant stays frozen until the first halving, then grants release one per day\\n  without unbonding; none releases until E.38 fixes the order. Only Agent grants may still spend\\n  locked principal on compute; a miner may bond its locked principal as its own self-stake.\\n- Make block rewards and the subsidy liquid on issue (R9.13; D-0518). The miner and validator legs and\\n  the Labs/Foundation subsidy are never auto-compounded, force-staked or vested, and agent-leg payouts\\n  are liquid once E.40 ratifies; validator earnings no longer auto-compound into locked stake, and E.39\\n  is closed.\\n\\n- Ratify \u00a78.2's grant-landing (R8.6) and claim and locked-spend (R8.8) rules as D-0515. D-0514\\n  had covered only R8.3\u2013R8.5 and R8.7; the requirement text is unchanged.\\n\\n- Repoint Appendix E blockers to live trackers and refresh Appendix H implementation grades for\\n  fee distribution, metering overflow, session-key invariants, and collusion monitoring.\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- Ratified D-0519 (owner, 2026-10-04): paid-storage service lanes close at a fixed boundary with\\n  a funding-pinned claim deadline on the finalized prefix. Only the lane's provider redeems, with\\n  strict sequence, counter and amount checks, including after reassignment. Otherwise-valid\\n  post-close signatures remain payable within the unspent budget until the deadline; finalization\\n  refunds only the remainder above it. Unknown finality rejects both operations. R5.4g, R5.4j,\\n  E.54, F.7 and G.4 carry the rule. E.54 constrains sequential entitlement-maturity and funded-request\\n  timing to fit that fixed deadline and requires an actual redeem-valid dispatch opportunity under\\n  permitted finality behavior before affected work can be enabled. Timing arithmetic alone does not\\n  establish it; the numerical window and production activation remain unratified.\\n\\n- Era-T conversion and airdrop vesting (\u00a78.2, R8.3\u2013R8.8; D-0514). Conversion draws only on the\\n  fixed genesis cohort pools. Agents are scored on settled compute-channel spend and miners on\\n  verified effective-FLOP; balance, stake, job count and active days carry no weight. Snapshot and\\n  evidence of record are fixed before genesis lock. Grants land as frozen genesis balances with\\n  linear vesting and finalized-head claims. The miner performance adjustment fails closed: without\\n  both performance inputs a Miner grant unlocks nothing before its end block. E.38 keeps score caps,\\n  the validator activity basis, the agent horizon and the remainder disposition open.\\n\\n- Development-network exception to the paid-storage activation gate (D-0512, R5.4q): a valueless\\n  devnet may run the \u00a75.4 extension ahead of E.54 only from a runtime built with a non-default,\\n  development-only flag, under an explicitly unratified profile with placeholder values kept out of\\n  the canonical parameters, with unaudited rent releases marked as such, and with no result citable\\n  as E.54/E.47 evidence. R5.4k now names R5.4q as its only exception; every other network is unchanged.\\n\\n- Paid encrypted agent memory/data storage specification (D-0506): fixed-term validator storage\\n  revenue, metered retrieval vouchers, scoped authority, versioned memory heads, and bounded\\n  audit/repair/refund obligations. Activation remains gated on E.54 economics/security calibration\\n  and E.47 availability evidence; protocol/model DA pricing is preserved. Distinguish client encryption\\n  from enforceable envelope validation, price setup separately from rent, and separate provider quotes\\n  from protocol limits and calibration inputs. Fund storage as take-or-pay capacity reservations:\\n  deletion frees bytes for reuse and refunds nothing, the remaining term is transferable, and rent\\n  rounds once per provider. Price by one epoch tariff (stake-weighted median of capacity-declaring\\n  validators, bounded step, under-supply tripwire) with protocol-random assignment among declarers.\\n  Keep the chain footprint flat: one reservation per payer, objects and ordinary memory heads in a\\n  provider-group manifest with quorum receipts and one checkpoint per provider per epoch, rent\\n  released per epoch without claims, setup and retrieval on one service lane per provider. Advance\\n  the retrieval request fee before the first chunk, calibrate audit sampling by `p_a \u00d7 c_r \u2265 c_s`,\\n  count held-back rent as bond. Bound attempted traffic and retries independently of voucher billing.\\n\\n- Initial normative specification of consensus, useful-inference verification, reference-work\\n  accounting, settlement, economics, data availability, and governance.\\n- Strict `< \u2153` Byzantine committee-seat premise with Lean quorum/nonforking references and explicit\\n  sampler, protocol-integration, and liveness assumptions.\\n- Reproducible committee counterexample, internal source-checked SCALE extrinsic fixtures, charged-weight\\n  ledger, negative capacity evidence, and conditional audit scenarios. Raw capacity-mechanism failure\\n  diagnostics and their reproducer remain private while E.46 is pending.\\n- Parameter and wire-format references, conformance status, and numbered open specification items.\\n- v0.5 decision D-0501 and proposed D-0502, pending ratification; D-0502 would withdraw unmeasured\\n  byte, throughput, and latency targets while earlier decisions retain their original IDs and history.\\n- D-0505 interoperable wire/rejection profile with explicit leaf and receipt versions, replay domains,\\n  legacy cutoffs, FCC4 transcripts, and a public Rust/TypeScript/Python vector corpus.\\n- D-0511 canonical compute-channel transcript/receipt definition, including per-channel decode-policy\\n  pinning and the path-specific `payable` derivation.\\n- D-0440 genesis sizing: a 4,400,000,000 FLOP genesis supply whose validator cohort is the aggregate\\n  1,200,000 FLOP bond across the 1,000-seat active-set cap.\\n- Proposed D-0507\u2013D-0510, pending ratification: post-testnet genesis validator selection and placement,\\n  floor-based rotation with an on-chain performance signal, committee sessions aligned to the BABE\\n  epoch, and bootstrap nodes.\\n- E.55 open item for a canonical pre-session quote/discovery contract.\\n- Whole-integer `G_n` representation and full-context cache accounting for profile v1.\\n- Publication checks for references, parameters, source layouts, generated artifacts, and\\n  offline sampler reproduction independent of the surrounding Python project's dependencies.\\n- Public standalone claim-ledger reproduction and canonical Appendix A parameter source/generator,\\n  with gates for published command paths, Python floors, and lossy parameter descriptions.\\n- Private Lean/Quint sources, locks, axiom checks, bounded model witnesses and mutation fixtures, plus\\n  a summary-only **PENDING** public profile; source licensing and implementation refinement must pass\\n  before the runnable bundle or broader protocol claims are published.\\n- Curated Lean sources and axiom checks with frozen source hashes, exact committee enumeration,\\n  and conditional audit/shared-collateral evidence; remaining publication and runtime-test gaps stay explicit.\\n- HTLC pair-qualification requirements with local-safety scope and an explicit PENDING disposition\\n  for end-to-end cross-chain conformance.\\n\",\"lastModified\":null},{\"source\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flop-labs/yellowpaper/70e691c758e53eed17a016a02b0a2ae1200d3744/decisions/v0.5.md\",\"status\":200,\"measuredAt\":\"2026-11-06T03:27:25.814Z\",\"body\":\"# FLOP Yellow Paper \u2014 Decision Record \u00b7 v0.5\\n\\nThis record accompanies the initial **v0.5.0 (draft)** public release. There has been no public\\nrelease of this paper yet; prepublication edits are folded into that baseline.\\n\\nThe [v0.4 decision record](v0.4.md) remains the immutable provenance for `D-0401`\u2013`D-0440`;\\n`D-0438`\u2013`D-0440` continue its genesis-sizing series and were recorded before publication.\\nThose decisions continue to apply except where a later decision explicitly supersedes their scope.\\nNew v0.5 decisions use `D-0501` onward. A decision's identifier is not the paper's patch version.\\nAfter a decision merges, revise it only through a new superseding ID.\\n\\n## Index\\n\\n| Decision | Scope | Status | Specification |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| [D-0501](#d-0501--public-claim-scope-and-reproducible-evidence) | Initial public claim scope, proof assumptions, evidence, and conformance | Draft for initial publication | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a70\u2013\u00a75, \u00a79\u2013\u00a715, Appendices E\u2013H |\\n| [D-0502](#d-0502--withdraw-unmeasured-capacity-and-latency-profile) | Capacity and latency claim disposition | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.2, \u00a75.2\u2013\u00a75.3, \u00a712.1, E.46 |\\n| [D-0505](#d-0505--canonical-wire-and-accounting-profile) | Canonical encoding, rejection, replay domains, legacy cutoff, and `G_n` representation | Draft for initial publication | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a74, Appendices F\u2013H |\\n| [D-0507](#d-0507--post-testnet-genesis-validator-selection-and-placement-admission) | Genesis composition of the first active set, placement admission, per-domain cap | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a715.2, \u00a715.5, E.41 |\\n| [D-0508](#d-0508--rotation-on-underperformance-floor-based-ejection-and-on-chain-performance-signal) | Floor-based ejection, on-chain performance signal, rotation throughput | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a715.4, \u00a715.5 |\\n| [D-0509](#d-0509--committee-session-cadence-aligned-to-the-babe-epoch) | `AlephEpochBlocks` 900 \u2192 BABE epoch (3,600) | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification; consensus safety zone | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.2, \u00a715.4 |\\n| [D-0510](#d-0510--bootstrap-nodes-and-the-public-discovery-plane) | Bootnodes as a launch requirement: count, placement, node keys, chain-spec wiring, unique `protocol_id` | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | bootnodes.md |\\n| [D-0506](#d-0506--paid-agent-memory-and-validator-data-services) | Paid encrypted application storage and retrieval, validator revenue, authority and liability | Draft; activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4, Appendices D\u2013H |\\n| [D-0511](#d-0511--one-canonical-compute-channel-transcript-and-receipt-definition) | One canonical compute-channel transcript and receipt definition | Draft for initial publication | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a76.5, \u00a712.1, Appendix C.3\u2013C.4, Appendix F.3 |\\n| [D-0512](#d-0512--development-network-exception-to-the-paid-storage-activation-gate) | Paid storage on development networks ahead of E.54 | Ratified 2026-09-25 (owner); development networks only | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4k, R5.4q; Appendices E.54, H.3 |\\n| [D-0513](#d-0513--one-generic-ss58-prefix-on-every-flop-network) | Account-address prefix: generic Substrate `42` on every FLOP network; networks told apart by genesis hash | Ratified 2026-09-29 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a76.5 R6.5a, \u00a79.3 R9.8 |\\n| [D-0514](#d-0514--era-t-conversion-rules-and-a-fail-closed-miner-adjustment) | Era-T conversion: supply, eligibility and scoring inputs, snapshot of record; the miner airdrop adjustment fails closed on missing performance data | Ratified 2026-09-29 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.3\u2013R8.5, R8.7; Appendices E.38, H.4 |\\n| [D-0515](#d-0515--airdrop-grant-landing-claims-and-locked-compute-spend) | Airdrop grants funded once as frozen genesis balances; finalized-head claims; locked principal spendable only on four compute calls | Ratified 2026-09-29 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.6, R8.8; Appendix H.4 |\\n| [D-0517](#d-0517--airdrop-unlock-miner-earn-to-unlock-validator-halving-release) | Miner grant a quarter at genesis, the rest 1:1 against finalized inference settlement; Validator grant frozen to the first halving, then one per day; locked Miner principal usable only as self-stake, locked Validator principal immobile | Ratified 2026-09-30 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.7, R8.8; Appendix E.38, H.4 |\\n| [D-0518](#d-0518--block-rewards-and-the-subsidy-are-liquid-on-issue) | Miner and validator block-reward legs and the Labs/Foundation subsidy liquid on issue, agent-leg payouts liquid once E.40 ratifies; validator earnings no longer auto-compound | Ratified 2026-09-30 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79 R9.2, R9.13; Appendix D, E.40, H.4 |\\n| [D-0519](#d-0519--service-lane-close-boundary-claim-window-and-provider-only-redemption) | Paid-storage service lanes: close boundary, a claim window on the finalized prefix, bounded post-close exposure, provider-only redemption | Ratified 2026-10-04 (owner); activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4g, R5.4j; Appendices E.54, F.7, G.4 |\\n| [D-0522](#d-0522--agent-airdrop-spend-to-unlock) | Agent grant unlocks one FLOP per three FLOP of locked principal in settled `payable`s, spend capped at three quarters of principal, locked-first payout and refund; no end block | Ratified 2026-10-05 (owner); spend cap and attribution await owner confirmation in review | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.7, R8.8; Appendices E.38, H.4 |\\n\\n| [D-0523](#d-0523--dormant-htlc-timeout-burn) | Default-zero HTLC burn implementation and proposed activation semantics | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification; not activated | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a710, E.48 |\\n\\n| [D-0524](#d-0524--slot-and-tranche-position-rent) | Rent rounding per slot and funding tranche, historical replacement entitlements and payer dust refunds | Ratified 2026-10-07 (owner); activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4e/f; Appendices E.54, F.7, G.4 |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0501 \u2014 Public claim scope and reproducible evidence\\n\\n**Status:** draft for initial public release. Defines the claim scope and evidence requirements of\\nv0.5.0; publication requires the prepublication evidence review. Runtime behavior, economic settings,\\nand cryptographic mechanisms are unchanged.\\n\\n**Context.** The first public paper needs a single, inspectable statement of its guarantees. Selection\\nstake and voting seats, artifact existence and proof applicability, signed work and verified execution,\\nand session call count and byte/work costs are separate quantities. Each claim needs explicit assumptions\\nand independently inspectable evidence. The finite-population counterexample and sizing calculations are reproduced in\\n[prepublication-evidence](../research/prepublication-evidence.md).\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. **Consensus:** use AlephBFT's strict `< \u2153` Byzantine-seat premise (`3f < n`) throughout the current\\n   specification. The Lean `Consensus.Committee.quorum_inter_gt_third` and `Consensus.FinalCtx.nonforking`\\n   proofs apply with one unit of voting weight per seat, quorum-certified finalization, and honest\\n   non-equivocation. Selection stake and voting seats are different quantities. Withdraw any assertion that general\\n   unequal-stake sampling of distinct identities automatically satisfies a binomial tail bound at the\\n   attacker's aggregate stake fraction. The mechanism-specific capture proof remains an explicit open\\n   obligation; no secure global concentration threshold is inferred from this correction.\\n2. **Verification:** qualify D-0432's soundness wording. TEE independence is a target trust-domain\\n   requirement, not evidence that a complete SOFT settlement/dispute lane exists. The abstract fallback\\n   theorem is conditional on its supplied verifier-error bound and enforcement mapping. D-0431's\\n   mandatory commitment remains; presence, signature/aggregate consistency, execution checks, and\\n   reward eligibility are stated separately. A multiplicative error model requires justified\\n   independence or suitable conditional bounds. Closed-channel reward recovery remains open.\\n3. **Finality:** D-0430's freeze/recovery policy governs stalled finality. A best-head payout claim does\\n   not follow from a theorem about undisputed channels on one modeled chain. Remove conflicting\\n   normative permission for best-head escape; do not change the deployed finality path.\\n4. **Accounting and sizing:** qualify D-0405's measurement language as the implemented reference-work\\n   convention; physical counters, cache-aware work, and E.30's unit taxonomy require their own evidence\\n   or definition. Correct direct-rail quorum arithmetic; the inherited 125,000-byte propagation budget\\n   is a sizing assumption pending workload/topology measurements. D-0427's lane limits are admission targets;\\n   their lifecycle conversion excludes extra evidence calls and is not a measured throughput result.\\n5. **Conditional security:** economic deterrence requires effective collectible monitoring and bounded\\n   total exposure; it is not a Byzantine soundness theorem or a full equilibrium from one inequality.\\n   DA custody, HTLC inclusion, governance-approved runtime changes, and external demand each retain\\n   explicit assumptions and unresolved obligations. Historical design rationale is not proof of those\\n   assumptions.\\n6. **Publication:** every claim states whether its backing is mathematical, modeled, measured, or\\n   assumed. Export a curated reproducible evidence bundle and distinguish private artifact existence\\n   from independent reproducibility. Specification and runtime conformance statuses remain separate.\\n\\n**Alternatives.** Keeping the existing prose would retain false implications. Changing consensus,\\nsettlement economics, or the verifier while repairing publication would require separate protocol\\nchoices and implementation verification. This decision instead states the guarantees the current\\nmodels support and makes the remaining engineering/research obligations reviewable.\\n\\n**Consequences.** This narrows claims, not admission or payment gates. The public draft is suitable for\\nreview of specified mechanisms and assumptions; it does not certify production security, benchmarked\\ncapacity, or completion of Appendix E. The documented session-key lifetime is corrected from the\\nunchanged 864,000-block runtime constant, with a regression gate for its day conversion.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** The unconditional security/measurement/sizing interpretations of D-0401,\\nD-0405, D-0427 and D-0432 described above; preserves their underlying protocol choices. Reaffirms\\nD-0430 for finality-sensitive settlement. No historical entry is rewritten.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0502 \u2014 Withdraw unmeasured capacity and latency profile\\n\\n**Status:** proposal pending ratification. Claim-scope change only; runtime limits, dispatch, and\\nconsensus behavior are unchanged. This record does not ratify the disposition.\\n\\n**Mechanism status:** PENDING. E.46 stays open until runtime benchmarks and the disclosed network\\nmatrix exist; partial encoding evidence does not close it.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0427 proposed 96 steady/128 burst session-control transactions per block and derived\\n48/64 cooperative lifecycles per second from two inclusions. The paper also carried a 125,000-byte\\npropagation scenario and sub-second finality target. None was established by serialized runtime calls,\\nsupported runtime benchmarks, or an end-to-end network/workload matrix. Deterministic SCALE fixtures\\nnow include actual runtime pallet/call indices, extrinsic framing, signature and extension fields, DA\\nreferences, and boundary turn/path inputs. They show byte growth but cannot establish throughput or\\nlatency.\\n\\n**Proposed decision.** If ratified, remove all four figures from the v0.5 conformance profile. State only the configured 5\\nMiB block-length limit (75% Normal) and two-dimensional runtime weight envelope, neither as a capacity\\nguarantee. Publish charged dispatch weights as estimates, not measurements: the direct-attestation\\nweight identifies itself as an unbenchmarked stub, and session weights do not parameterize Merkle-path\\nlength or duplicate scanning. Keep E.46 open until the runtime-benchmark build succeeds and the\\ndisclosed network matrix reports successful, rejected, backlogged, and stalled runs. A future capacity\\nprofile requires a new decision and must distinguish chain admission, serving capacity, and\\nclient-perceived latency.\\n\\n**Evidence.** [Prepublication evidence](../research/prepublication-evidence.md) and its\\n[JSON](../evidence/yellowpaper-claim-evidence.json) / [CSV](../evidence/yellowpaper-claim-evidence.csv)\\nbundle. The recorded runtime-benchmark attempt remains blocked by #550: its WASM dependency graph\\nmixes Polkadot SDK generations, producing cross-version crypto/type API failures and incompatible\\n`sp-io` 38.0.2/48.0.0 implementations that abort on duplicate `panic_impl`. Therefore no runtime/build\\nhash or benchmark output exists. No network run was performed; the empty log/hash/hardware fields and\\nrequired workload matrix are retained as negative evidence.\\n\\n**Consequences if ratified.** The paper makes no finalized-lifecycle throughput or inclusion/finality percentile\\nclaim. Runtime admission continues to enforce its existing byte, weight, state, and validity gates.\\nMeasured profiles may be added only with raw logs, runtime/build hashes, generator and seeds, hardware\\nprofile, configuration, repetitions, uncertainty, and rerun instructions.\\n\\n**Would supersede by scope.** D-0427's throughput/admission figures and D-0501's temporary treatment of the\\n125,000-byte and latency figures as targets. Preserves D-0401's one-second BABE slot and every deployed\\nruntime limit.\\n\\n## D-0505 \u2014 Canonical wire and accounting profile\\n\\n**Status:** draft for initial public release; runtime and Rust/TypeScript/Python SDK changes are implemented\\nin the authoring repository. Publication remains subject to approval of the exact public bundle.\\n\\n**Context.** Appendix F previously summarized fields but left widths, container prefixes, version dispatch,\\ndomain separation, malformed input policy, tree behavior, and migration cutoffs implicit. The runtime tried\\nmultiple historical leaf hashes after signature failure, making the accepted format set difficult to audit.\\n`G_n` also lacked one numeric taxonomy across the direct and session rails.\\n\\n**Decision.** Adopt Appendix F's `flop-wire-v1` profile and its public byte-for-byte corpus.\\n\\n1. Fixed integers are unsigned little-endian; SCALE enums/options/bools and compact lengths are canonical\\n   and fail closed. Objects reject unknown tags, invalid presence values, truncation, overflow, overlong\\n   compact integers, and trailing bytes.\\n2. Direct-rail task producers use the versioned `FLOP/POUI/TASK` preimage. The runtime continues to receive\\n   its H256 as an opaque local replay key, so active validators must enforce that producer derivation before\\n   signing. Compute-channel IDs bind the genesis hash, parties, and nonce under a versioned protocol domain;\\n   receipts use their own domain/version and inherit deployment/session binding through the channel ID.\\n3. Every current settlement/dispute turn carries a `V0`\u2013`V3` SCALE tag. Verification selects exactly that\\n   preimage and never retries. Current policy-pinned channels accept V2/V3; pre-policy channels may explicitly\\n   select V0/V1. V2 is described as lower evidence and cannot be presented as V3/TOPLOC-bound assurance\\n   (#1404); policy-less legacy formats remain confined by the existing marker (#686).\\n4. Current DA transcripts use FCC4 with an explicit per-turn leaf version, strict optional-field consistency,\\n   and exact byte consumption. Historical unprefixed/FCC2/FCC3 reads are migration-only and produce one\\n   explicit in-memory version before verification.\\n5. New channels require the tagged receipt. A new `ChannelWireProfiles=1` marker is the cutoff: only absent\\n   pre-upgrade channels accept the historical untagged receipt, while unknown values fail closed. No channel\\n   storage value, amount, leaf preimage, or historical signature is rewritten.\\n6. Settled `G_n` is whole `floor(F_eff/10\u2079)`: `u64` on the direct rail and `u128` for sessions, with checked\\n   conversion/aggregation. Profile v1 bills the full prompt/context term even when a KV-cache prefix is reused.\\n   Any proof-bound cache discount requires a new meter/profile version (#1153), never reinterpretation.\\n\\n**Alternatives.** A leading byte inside every historical leaf would invalidate existing roots and signatures.\\nContinuing trial verification would preserve ambiguity. An additive wire-profile map distinguishes new opens\\nwithout rewriting in-flight channel storage.\\n\\n**Consequences.** E.30 closes; E.51 remains partial. The fixture publishes schema, inputs, exact\\nbytes/hashes/signature, positive and malformed/domain/version cases, generation commands, and consumer\\npaths. Production producer/validator enforcement of `task_hash` v1 and cross-version channel-resume receipt\\nselection remain open in E.51. V2's unbound TOPLOC field and proof-bound cache discounts remain separately\\nvisible under #1404 and #1153/E.22.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** Appendix F's unversioned receipt and trial-dispatch descriptions, and D-0501's\\nplaceholder treatment of the `G_n` numeric taxonomy. Historical V0\u2013V3 hash preimages remain unchanged.\\n\\n## D-0506 \u2014 Paid agent memory and validator data services\\n\\n**Status:** draft for initial publication, implementing the requested paid-memory product direction.\\nProtocol profile and numerical settings remain subject to E.54 ratification and implementation;\\nthis decision does not activate a runtime fee.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0412 makes DA a validator duty with no byte-fee revenue. Its two retention classes cover\\nprotocol evidence and leased model images. Agents also need private, versioned application storage\\nand must pay the validators retaining and returning those bytes.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. Add a separate paid application-object namespace, retaining sovereign validator custody and\\n   FLOP-denominated transfers. Preserve existing protocol/model duties and `DataRef` v1 encoding.\\n   FLOP is not a storage chain: keep on chain only the epoch tariff and capacity declarations, one\\n   reservation per payer, service lanes, one checkpoint per provider per epoch, and disputes. Object\\n   commitments, manifests and ordinary memory heads live with the assigned provider group, sequenced\\n   by the generation-order sequencer and quorum-receipted; heads reach the chain only through\\n   checkpoint roots or an optional owner pin. Agents, sessions and sub-agents operate inside one\\n   payer's reservation through delegated capabilities anchored in agent-wallet limits.\\n2. Fund storage as a fixed-term capacity reservation of reserved encoded bytes on a protocol-assigned\\n   provider set, priced by one epoch tariff: a per-write setup fee plus rent on reserved byte-blocks.\\n   The tariff is the stake-weighted median of the votes of validators that declare capacity for the\\n   epoch, clamped to a per-epoch step bound and any governance floor/ceiling, and stepped up when\\n   declared capacity stays below demand headroom. Assignment is a stake-weighted random sample among\\n   declarers; the payer cannot choose providers. Release rent per epoch against accepted audit and\\n   checkpoint state, rounded once per provider; earn setup at each write's activation receipts;\\n   include audit/repair/checkpoint overhead and bound liability. A reservation is take-or-pay:\\n   deleting an object never refunds or settles anything, it frees the bytes for reuse within the same\\n   term; the remaining term is transferable to another payer by signed consent. Keep\\n   failed-activation/provider-fault remedies, unused lane budgets and refundable deposits separate.\\n   Repair cannot repeat setup. Voting is not proof of costs; voluntary capacity and the step rule are\\n   the clearing mechanism.\\n3. Pay setup and retrieval through one funded service lane per assigned provider, shared by every\\n   namespace and delegate, using payer-signed cumulative vouchers over activated writes, verified\\n   encoded bytes and requests at the lane's pinned tariff version. No log-based debits, double\\n   redemption, new issuance, inference reward credit, or automatic committee-duty credit. The request\\n   fee is advanced with the request, before the first chunk, so the unpaid first chunk is no longer\\n   a free good.\\n   Retrieval pricing controls demand for validator traffic; it does not prove traffic safety by itself.\\n   Require direct authenticated pull, bounded retries and atomic aggregate work/egress/credit budgets\\n   that survive lane churn and restart, including failed admission and repair. Protect protocol reserves;\\n   fees recycled within a payer/provider coalition are not an external attack cost. No automatic burn\\n   or new protocol take is introduced.\\n4. Require conforming clients to encrypt private objects; validators enforce the envelope, commitments,\\n   signatures and authority, without claiming to prove encryption, secret/random keys or plaintext\\n   absence for arbitrary uploaders. Authorize operations with attenuated capabilities and wallet limits.\\n   Use immutable versions and compare-and-swap heads with a provider-quorum receipt, equivocation as\\n   an objective slashable fault, and an option"
